    "description": "<p>This paper investigates the use of simple local linear regression to predict mortality rates based on the percentage of families living in poverty. The study formulates local regression as a weighted least-squares optimization problem and examines three different weighting schemes based on the distance between observations. The influence of the number of neighboring observations is also explored, highlighting its effect on the fitted regression and prediction accuracy. Using selected neighborhood sizes, mortality rates are estimated for poverty levels of 10%, 18%, and 25%, together with their standard errors. The results demonstrate how different weighting strategies and neighborhood choices affect the local regression estimates, fitted trends, and associated uncertainty.</p>",
